concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sch-1-sec-16 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "16 Issuing authority"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sch-1-sec-16 ;
    values:originalText "(1) The Attorney-General may, by writing, appoint as an issuing authority:  (a) a person who is:  (i) a judge of a court created by the Parliament; or  (ii) a magistrate;  and in relation to whom a consent under subclause (2) is in force; or  International production orders  Schedule 1 Introduction  Part 1  Clause 17  Telecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979  Compilation date: 04/06/2026  (b) a person who:  (i) holds an appointment to the Administrative Review Tribunal as Deputy President, senior member or general member; and  (ii) is enrolled as a legal practitioner of a federal court or of the Supreme Court of a State or a Territory; and  (iii) has been so enrolled for at least 5 years.  (2) A person who is:  (a) a judge of a court created by the Parliament; or  (b) a magistrate; may, by writing, consent to be appointed by the Attorney-General under subclause (1).  (3) A person’s appointment ceases to have effect if the person ceases to be a person whom the Attorney-General could appoint under this clause.  (4) An appointment under subclause (1) has no effect for the purposes of a provision of this Act (other than this Schedule).  (5) An issuing authority has, in relation to the performance or exercise of a function or power conferred on an issuing authority by this Schedule, the same protection and immunity as a Justice of the High Court has in relation to proceedings in the High Court." ;
    skos:definition "(1) The Attorney-General may, by writing, appoint as an issuing authority:  (a) a person who is:  (i) a judge of a court created by the Parliament; or  (ii) a magistrate;  and in relation to whom a consent under subclause (2) is in force; or  International production orders  Schedule 1 Introduction  Part 1  Clause 17  Telecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979  Compilation date: 04/06/2026  (b) a person who:  (i) holds an appointment to the Administrative Review Tribunal as Deputy President, senior member or general member; and  (ii) is enrolled as a legal practitioner of a federal court or of the Supreme Court of a State or a Territory; and  (iii) has been so enrolled for at least 5 years.  (2) A person who is:  (a) a judge of a court created by the Parliament; or  (b) a magistrate; may, by writing, consent to be appointed by the Attorney-General under subclause (1).  (3) A person’s appointment ceases to have effect if the person ceases to be a person whom the Attorney-General could appoint under this clause.  (4) An appointment under subclause (1) has no effect for the purposes of a provision of this Act (other than this Schedule).  (5) An issuing authority has, in relation to the performance or exercise of a function or power conferred on an issuing authority by this Schedule, the same protection and immunity as a Justice of the High Court has in relation to proceedings in the High Court."@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "00706b20774c5113e250d653482b61ba7f7cb613625a3b6e311bb4f6787d8eaf" ;
    cof:pageNumber "94"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sch-1-sec-16-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sch-1-sec-16-ss-2 ;
