concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sch-1-sec-126 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "126 Civil penalty provision—enforcement"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sch-1-sec-126 ;
    values:originalText "Enforceable civil penalty provision  (1) A civil penalty provision in this Part is enforceable under Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers (Standard Provisions) Act 2014. Note: Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers (Standard Provisions) Act 2014 allows a civil penalty provision to be enforced by obtaining an order for a person to pay a pecuniary penalty for the contravention of the provision. Authorised applicant  (2) For the purposes of Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers (Standard Provisions) Act 2014, a Communications Access Coordinator is an authorised applicant in relation to a civil penalty provision in this Part. Relevant court  (3) For the purposes of Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers (Standard Provisions) Act 2014, the Federal Court of Australia and the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia are relevant courts in relation to a civil penalty provision in this Part. Penalty for a body corporate  (4) Paragraph 82(5)(a) of the Regulatory Powers (Standard Provisions) Act 2014, as it applies in relation to a civil penalty provision in this Part, has effect as if “5 times” were omitted and “200 times” were substituted. Extra-territorial application  (5) Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers (Standard Provisions) Act 2014, as it applies in relation to a civil penalty provision in this Part, extends to acts, omissions, matters and things outside Australia.  International production orders  Schedule 1 Reporting and record-keeping requirements  Part 9 Introduction  Division 1  Clause 127  Telecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979  Compilation date: 04/06/2026" ;
    skos:definition "Enforceable civil penalty provision  (1) A civil penalty provision in this Part is enforceable under Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers (Standard Provisions) Act 2014. Note: Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers (Standard Provisions) Act 2014 allows a civil penalty provision to be enforced by obtaining an order for a person to pay a pecuniary penalty for the contravention of the provision. Authorised applicant  (2) For the purposes of Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers (Standard Provisions) Act 2014, a Communications Access Coordinator is an authorised applicant in relation to a civil penalty provision in this Part. Relevant court  (3) For the purposes of Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers (Standard Provisions) Act 2014, the Federal Court of Australia and the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia are relevant courts in relation to a civil penalty provision in this Part. Penalty for a body corporate  (4) Paragraph 82(5)(a) of the Regulatory Powers (Standard Provisions) Act 2014, as it applies in relation to a civil penalty provision in this Part, has effect as if “5 times” were omitted and “200 times” were substituted. Extra-territorial application  (5) Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers (Standard Provisions) Act 2014, as it applies in relation to a civil penalty provision in this Part, extends to acts, omissions, matters and things outside Australia.  International production orders  Schedule 1 Reporting and record-keeping requirements  Part 9 Introduction  Division 1  Clause 127  Telecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979  Compilation date: 04/06/2026"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "8c3e0c08707d8941deaa72f2020eff85449fc506d9124a9d617f51e756c19994" ;
    cof:pageNumber "226"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sch-1-sec-126-ss-1 ;
